# TOMS 748 root finding

TOMS Algorithm 748 solves a scalar equation `f(x) = 0` using higher-order
interpolation while contracting a sign-changing interval. It needs only
function values; the polynomial calculations do not require user derivatives.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/root/toms748/struct.Toms748Root.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                               |
  | ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Equation    | A continuous, deterministic scalar function                |
  | Evaluations | A fallible function-value callback                        |
  | Search      | One root inside a supplied bracket                        |
  | Interval    | Finite, strictly ordered endpoints with a sign change      |
  | Parameters  | Scalar `f32` or `f64`, without optional features            |

## When to use it

Consider TOMS 748 for smooth scalar functions with expensive evaluations and
a known bracket. Its interpolation cycles aim to reduce both root error and
bracket width quickly. Compare [Brent](../brent/) using function-evaluation
counts on your problem; an iteration has a different cost in the two methods.

For a simple baseline, compare [bisection](../bisection/). If first or second
derivatives are inexpensive, compare [Newton](../newton/) or
[Halley](../halley/). All require a sign-changing interval or an exact
endpoint root. An interior root of even multiplicity generally has no sign
change, and several enclosed roots do not select a particular solution.

## How it works

Basin implements Algorithm 4.2 from [Alefeld, Potra, and Shi
(1995)](#ref-alefeld-potra-shi-1995), with `k = 2` and `μ = 0.5`.
A startup secant step is followed by cycles containing two inverse cubic or
Newton-quadratic interpolations and a double-length secant step. If a cycle
fails to halve the interval, bisection enforces the contraction.

Newton-quadratic interpolation solves a polynomial model built from sampled
values; it does not evaluate the function's derivative. Basin scales function
values before interpolation. Repeated values, non-finite intermediate
arithmetic, or exterior proposals lead to lower-order interpolation or
bisection.

Each accepted sample updates the bracket by sign. An exact zero collapses it
immediately; otherwise, the returned estimate is the evaluated endpoint with
the smaller absolute residual.

## Example

This value-only callback solves `x² - 2 = 0` on `[0, 2]`. The iteration limit
counts startup and interpolation cycles, rather than individual callbacks.

<!-- compile: root-toms748 -->
```rust
use basin::Toms748Root;
use std::convert::Infallible;

fn main() {
    let result = Toms748Root::new(0.0, 2.0)
        .with_absolute_position_tolerance(1e-10)
        .with_max_iter(100)
        .solve(|x| Ok::<_, Infallible>(x * x - 2.0))
        .unwrap();

    assert!(result.converged());
    assert!((result.root() - 2.0_f64.sqrt()).abs() < 1e-10);
    assert_eq!(result.value(), result.root() * result.root() - 2.0);
    assert_eq!(result.function_evals(), result.callback_evals());
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Check the function contract

The endpoints must be finite and strictly ordered, with finite width and
opposite function signs unless one endpoint is exactly a root. The callback
must be deterministic and continuous on the interval, and finite wherever
evaluated. A sign change across a discontinuity does not establish a zero.

If the bracket is unknown,
[`RootBracketer`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/bracket/root/struct.RootBracketer.html)
can search geometrically within optional limits. Check `bracketed()` before
using its endpoints; the [Brent example](../brent/#example) shows the full
sequence. Search and solve evaluation counts are separate.

### Set position accuracy

Convergence requires an exact zero or a bracket width at most
`absolute + relative * abs(root)`. The defaults are absolute position
tolerance `1e-12` and relative position tolerance `4ε` for the scalar type.
Set them with `with_absolute_position_tolerance` and
`with_relative_position_tolerance`.

Absolute tolerance must be finite and positive, and relative tolerance must
be finite and at least `4ε`; invalid tolerance settings panic. The bracket
test controls position accuracy under the function contract. Inspect the
signed `value()` separately if your application needs a residual tolerance.
For `f32`, choose accuracy consistent with its larger machine epsilon.

### Compare evaluations rather than iterations

`iterations()` counts the startup step and subsequent cycles that evaluate
at least one point. A full cycle can use four function evaluations and can
stop early on convergence. The default limit of 100 iterations is therefore
not a 100-evaluation budget. Use `function_evals()` to measure callback work.

`with_max_iter(0)` evaluates only endpoints, stopping after the first if it
is an exact root. Exhausting the limit returns `Ok` with
`RootTerminationReason::MaxIter`; inspect `converged()` or `reason()` before
using the estimate. Invalid intervals, missing sign changes, non-finite
function values, and application errors return typed `RootError` variants.

## Implementation

Each `solve` starts afresh and reevaluates its endpoints. `RootResult`
retains the estimate, signed value, final ordered bracket, termination reason,
and work counts. `function_evals()` includes endpoints and equals
`callback_evals()`; derivative counts are zero.

Scalar `f32` and `f64` work without a linear-algebra backend or optional
feature, including on WASM. Final-output reference checks use SciPy 1.16.3
with `k = 2`. Floating-point safeguards and stopping rules differ, so general
trajectory parity is not claimed.

